From 04852a51bee67d5979bc3e2aec16ef665bebf327 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 08:34:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08886/11362] Add sensitivity to goal-predicate scoring; the
 live veto is inverted at level 1

METRIC FIX. score_goal_predicate_consistency's accuracy is a SPECIFICITY score:
held-out transitions contain no level-ups, so a predicate returning False for
everything is 100% correct. It now takes an optional win_grids and returns
sensitivity_win_grids_tested, sensitivity_win_grids_fired and
is_degenerate_constant_false, so the constant-False predicate can no longer be
indistinguishable from a good one. Additive and optional: defaults unchanged, no
existing caller moves, and with no win grid supplied the flag is None
(UNMEASURABLE) rather than a pass -- "nobody checked" must not look like "checked
and fine". 11 tests, including the constant-True opposite degeneracy and a
backward-compatibility case.

One test was written wrong and the code was right: a predicate that RAISES also
raises in the specificity loop, so it scores 0.0 and is already failing loudly.
The degenerate flag is reserved for the SILENT case -- high specificity, no fire --
which is the one nothing else catches. Test corrected to assert that.

THE BIGGER FINDING, a code-path analysis rather than a live observation. Every
step verified in source or by test; the conclusion follows by construction:

  1. The live agent sets min_goal_predicate_consistency=1.0 at both reinduction
     call sites (arc_competition_agent.py:7164 and :7309).
  2. arc_llm_reinduction rejects a candidate when accuracy < threshold, so a
     candidate must score EXACTLY 1.0 to survive.
  3. The scored window is the agent's OWN transitions.
  4. Before the agent's first level-up, no transition in that window can have
     level_after > level_before.
  5. On such a window a constant-False predicate scores exactly 1.0.

So at level 1 on a hidden game -- the moment the agent must break through, and
where no win exemplar can exist -- the veto ACCEPTS a constant-False predicate and
REJECTS a discriminating one that fires even once too often. It selects for the
useless predicate at the only moment that matters. plan_in_model then terminates
on that predicate, which is exactly the pattern exp6257 measured: 10 of 14
degenerate games found no plan and 4 produced verified hollow plans. It also
explains exp6256's 8 of 8 freshly induced predicates never firing on a real win.

From level 2 the window can contain a real level-up and the inversion relaxes, so
this is a level-1 cold-start defect specifically -- which is where the live agent's
scored performance is stuck.

DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED: the live threshold. Lowering it, or rewiring the veto to
consult the new flag, is a behaviour change on the scored agent and should be a
measured A/B and an operator decision, not a quiet edit at the end of an
unattended session. This analysis is the case for running that A/B.

